# Copy to config.env and fill in. Precedence: caller env > config.env > defaults,
# so any value here can be overridden for a single run:
#   OUTPUT_DIR=/tmp/out ./madrun.sh
# Relative paths resolve against the repo root. config.env is git-ignored.

# Directory containing the `apptainer` binary. The only value most setups need.
APPTAINER_DIR=""

# Directory containing the `opencode` binary, for a `--provider opencode` run
# (see local/README.md — opencode is how a self-hosted model is reached). Same
# shape as APPTAINER_DIR above: the directory, not the executable.
#
# Worth setting, because opencode ships as a single downloaded binary that
# frequently lives outside PATH, and this repository deliberately does not
# vendor it. Left empty, the launcher looks on PATH, then in the usual install
# prefixes, and finally installs opencode inside the container.
# Set OPENCODE_BIN instead if your executable has a non-standard name.
OPENCODE_DIR=""

# Container image, built by image/create_image.sh into its type's folder
# (image/preinstall/madagents.sif, image/minimal/..., image/clean/...). Leave
# empty to run whichever type is built, preferring the fullest stack; set it to
# pick one when several are built, or to use an image from a shared location.
APPTAINER_IMAGE=""

# Writable overlay. Leave empty: each run instance gets its own sparse overlay at
# <instance>/overlay.img, which is what lets instances run concurrently. Setting
# it here points every instance at one file, which they cannot safely share.
APPTAINER_OVERLAY=""

# Where a bare run puts deliverables and runtime state. A run instance sets these
# to <instance>/output and <instance>/run, so they apply only outside the harness.
OUTPUT_DIR=""
RUN_DIR=""

# Claude Code config dir, bind-mounted to /opt/.config/.claude in the container.
# Effectively required, and worth pointing at your normal one (e.g. ~/.claude):
# it is where the session's login and its accepted-trust state live. Left empty,
# the launcher cannot pre-accept the workspace-trust dialog and every run
# instance starts from a fresh overlay with no config of its own.
# Note the container reads your settings.json from here — a project-level
# setting the run needs (auto-memory) is pinned per instance so your own
# settings cannot silently turn it off.
CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=""

# The same thing for a `--provider codex` run: your Codex home (e.g. ~/.codex),
# bind-mounted to /opt/.config/.codex. Required for a Codex run for the same two
# reasons — it holds the login, and the launcher writes the container's trust
# entries there, without which Codex ignores the project's .codex/ layer and the
# 46 consultants silently do not load.
# It is also what makes an approval stick: when you add a command to the allow
# list, Codex writes a rule to <CODEX_HOME>/rules/default.rules, and binding this
# directory is what carries that rule from one run to the next. Left empty, every
# run starts unauthenticated and re-asks for everything.
CODEX_HOME=""

# The same idea for opencode, but for different reasons — opencode has no trust
# step, and on a self-hosted endpoint its credentials come from the generated
# per-run config rather than from a login. One directory holding opencode's four
# XDG roots (config, data, cache, state), bind-mounted so runs share them.
#
# Worth setting for two things it caches: the ~3.4 MB model catalogue opencode
# downloads at startup — which a compute node without network egress cannot
# fetch, so a populated cache is what lets it start at all — and your session
# history. Left empty, every run starts with empty roots inside the container.
OPENCODE_HOME=""

# opencode only: opt in to the `websearch` tool. Empty (the default) means the
# tool does not exist in the session at all.
#
# opencode hides websearch unless OPENCODE_ENABLE_EXA is set, which this sets
# for you. It costs nothing and needs no API key — the keyless path talks to
# Exa AI's hosted service unauthenticated — but that is exactly why it is off
# by default here:
#
#   - every query leaves your network, to a third party you have no contract
#     with. On a self-hosted deployment that is usually the thing you were
#     avoiding;
#   - the free path is rate-limited by an undocumented quota (opencode issue
#     #15953 reports "Exa hit rate limit"), so it fails INTERMITTENTLY rather
#     than cleanly;
#   - it needs general internet egress, which a compute node may not have.
#
# Worth knowing either way: `ma-physics-consultant` and `ma-numerics-consultant`
# are both instructed to verify literature citations and PDG constants by web
# search rather than from recall. With this off they find no such tool and fall
# back to recall — the very thing those cards exist to prevent. With it on they
# can search, but a rate-limited miss puts them back there silently. Neither
# state is free; pick the one your setup can live with.
OPENCODE_ENABLE_WEBSEARCH=""

# Apptainer's own config dir (passed through to apptainer).
APPTAINER_CONFIGDIR=""

# SLURM passthrough. Empty or "auto" binds /etc/slurm + the munge socket when
# this host looks like a submit host, so the container's sbatch/squeue reach the
# same controller. "0" never binds; "1" requires it and fails the launch if the
# host has no SLURM. Note this binds the scheduler only — no shared scratch is
# provisioned and compute nodes do not get the container's software stack; both
# are site-specific and left to you. Other schedulers are not integrated.
BIND_SLURM=""

# NOTE: no API keys here. Claude Code authenticates itself (subscription or API
# credits), and the launcher actively STRIPS OPENAI_API_KEY / ANTHROPIC_API_KEY /
# LLM_API_KEY and friends from the container environment. The API-key-driven
# release lives in legacy/madagents_v1/ with its own config.env.example.
